1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,790
A bowl of rice, a spoon
of ghee, a cup of dal.

2
00:00:03,790 --> 00:00:05,560
Three very different foods.

3
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:10,469
Yet your cells can burn all three
for energy, in one shared pathway.

4
00:00:10,919 --> 00:00:13,379
Diya wondered about this in the kitchen.

5
00:00:13,379 --> 00:00:17,211
She asked: how much energy does
one sugar molecule really give?

6
00:00:17,211 --> 00:00:19,966
And does every food use
oxygen the same way?

7
00:00:20,316 --> 00:00:21,929
Here is the short answer.

8
00:00:21,929 --> 00:00:24,133
One glucose can give thirty-eight ATP.

9
00:00:24,133 --> 00:00:26,580
And the gas trade changes with the food.

10
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:30,734
By the end, you will know the
respiratory balance sheet, the amphibolic

11
00:00:30,734 --> 00:00:32,796
pathway, and the respiratory quotient.

12
00:00:33,446 --> 00:00:35,246
First, what is a balance sheet?

13
00:00:35,596 --> 00:00:39,814
A balance sheet counts what comes in and
what goes out, like a bank statement.

14
00:00:40,264 --> 00:00:44,383
For respiration, we count the ATP
made from one glucose molecule.

15
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:46,375
ATP is the cell's energy coin.

16
00:00:46,375 --> 00:00:48,116
Glucose and oxygen go in.

17
00:00:48,116 --> 00:00:50,746
Carbon dioxide, water and ATP come out.

18
00:00:51,396 --> 00:00:54,286
Let's zoom in to where
most of this ATP is made.

19
00:00:54,636 --> 00:00:57,139
A plant cell has many mitochondria.

20
00:00:57,139 --> 00:01:00,607
Glycolysis happens outside
them, in the cytoplasm.

21
00:01:01,107 --> 00:01:02,111
Zoom closer.

22
00:01:02,111 --> 00:01:06,790
Inside one mitochondrion, the Krebs
cycle runs in the matrix, the jelly

23
00:01:06,790 --> 00:01:07,960
in the middle.

24
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:10,105
Zoom to the inner membrane.

25
00:01:10,105 --> 00:01:13,889
Here the electron transport system
turns NADH and FADH two into ATP.

26
00:01:13,889 --> 00:01:15,492
Each NADH gives three ATP.

27
00:01:15,492 --> 00:01:17,007
Each FADH two gives two.

28
00:01:17,657 --> 00:01:20,610
But this count works only
if four things are true.

29
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:21,694
Let's check them.

30
00:01:22,044 --> 00:01:22,944
One.

31
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:27,859
The pathway runs in order: glycolysis,
then the Krebs cycle, then the electron

32
00:01:27,859 --> 00:01:29,140
transport system.

33
00:01:29,890 --> 00:01:30,790
Two.

34
00:01:30,279 --> 00:01:33,769
The NADH made in glycolysis enters
the mitochondria and makes ATP there.

35
00:01:34,519 --> 00:01:35,419
Three.

36
00:01:35,051 --> 00:01:38,449
No middle product leaves the
pathway to build something else.

37
00:01:39,199 --> 00:01:40,099
Four.

38
00:01:39,714 --> 00:01:41,314
Only glucose is respired.

39
00:01:41,314 --> 00:01:43,153
No other food enters halfway.

40
00:01:43,903 --> 00:01:48,168
If all four hold, one glucose gives
a net gain of thirty-eight ATP.

41
00:01:48,168 --> 00:01:51,595
But in a living cell these
rules are not always true.

42
00:01:51,595 --> 00:01:54,012
So thirty-eight is a calculated figure.

43
00:01:54,762 --> 00:01:58,883
Now compare this with fermentation,
which runs without oxygen.

44
00:01:59,233 --> 00:02:02,011
Fermentation breaks glucose only partly.

45
00:02:02,011 --> 00:02:06,858
Aerobic respiration breaks it
completely, to carbon dioxide and water.

46
00:02:07,308 --> 00:02:11,116
Fermentation gives a net gain
of only two ATP per glucose.

47
00:02:11,116 --> 00:02:13,464
Aerobic respiration gives many more.

48
00:02:13,914 --> 00:02:17,683
And in fermentation, NADH turns
back into NAD plus slowly.

49
00:02:17,683 --> 00:02:20,590
In aerobic respiration
this happens very fast.

50
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:24,180
Now, rule three said
nothing leaves the pathway.

51
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:26,901
In a real cell, things leave all the time.

52
00:02:27,251 --> 00:02:28,982
Glucose is the favourite fuel.

53
00:02:28,982 --> 00:02:31,372
But fats and proteins can be respired too.

54
00:02:31,822 --> 00:02:34,524
Fats first split into
glycerol and fatty acids.

55
00:02:34,524 --> 00:02:36,322
Fatty acids become acetyl CoA.

56
00:02:36,322 --> 00:02:39,549
Glycerol becomes PGAL, a
three-carbon sugar of glycolysis.

57
00:02:39,999 --> 00:02:41,978
Proteins split into amino acids.

58
00:02:41,978 --> 00:02:46,331
Each one enters as pyruvate, as
acetyl CoA, or inside the Krebs cycle.

59
00:02:46,781 --> 00:02:48,773
Breaking food down is catabolism.

60
00:02:48,773 --> 00:02:50,901
Building new molecules is anabolism.

61
00:02:50,901 --> 00:02:55,012
When the cell needs fatty acids, it
pulls acetyl CoA out of the pathway

62
00:02:55,012 --> 00:02:56,035
to build them.

63
00:02:56,485 --> 00:02:59,330
So one pathway both
breaks down and builds up.

64
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:03,786
That is why it is called an amphibolic
pathway, not just a catabolic one.

65
00:03:04,436 --> 00:03:06,179
Now, the third idea.

66
00:03:06,179 --> 00:03:09,314
Respiration trades oxygen
for carbon dioxide.

67
00:03:09,664 --> 00:03:14,468
The respiratory quotient, or RQ, is
the volume of carbon dioxide given

68
00:03:14,468 --> 00:03:17,456
out divided by the volume of oxygen used.

69
00:03:17,906 --> 00:03:22,737
For glucose, six carbon dioxide
come out for six oxygen taken in.

70
00:03:22,737 --> 00:03:24,873
Six divided by six is one.

71
00:03:25,323 --> 00:03:29,336
For a fat like tripalmitin, two
molecules give out one hundred and two

72
00:03:29,336 --> 00:03:32,587
carbon dioxide and use one
hundred and forty-five oxygen.

73
00:03:32,587 --> 00:03:34,689
The RQ is about zero point seven.

74
00:03:35,139 --> 00:03:37,916
For proteins, the RQ is
about zero point nine.

75
00:03:37,916 --> 00:03:39,753
Real cells usually burn a mix.

76
00:03:39,753 --> 00:03:42,713
Pure fats or pure proteins
are never the only fuel.

77
00:03:43,163 --> 00:03:44,063
Your turn.

78
00:03:43,994 --> 00:03:45,665
A seed burns mostly fat.

79
00:03:45,665 --> 00:03:48,578
Is its RQ more than one, or less than one?

80
00:03:48,578 --> 00:03:49,801
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:53,001 --> 00:03:54,116
Less than one!

82
00:03:54,116 --> 00:03:58,635
Fats hold little oxygen, so they need
more oxygen for each carbon dioxide

83
00:03:58,635 --> 00:03:59,641
they release.

84
00:04:00,291 --> 00:04:01,409
Remember this.

85
00:04:01,409 --> 00:04:04,796
RQ is carbon dioxide out,
divided by oxygen in.

86
00:04:05,396 --> 00:04:06,874
Now, a worked example.

87
00:04:06,874 --> 00:04:09,612
Let's count the thirty-eight
ATP from one glucose.

88
00:04:09,962 --> 00:04:12,203
Glycolysis gives two ATP and two NADH.

89
00:04:12,203 --> 00:04:13,956
Two NADH times three is six.

90
00:04:13,956 --> 00:04:15,395
Two plus six is eight.

91
00:04:15,845 --> 00:04:18,839
The link step, pyruvate to
acetyl CoA, gives two NADH.

92
00:04:18,839 --> 00:04:20,404
Two times three is six ATP.

93
00:04:20,854 --> 00:04:22,571
The Krebs cycle turns twice.

94
00:04:22,571 --> 00:04:27,196
Two ATP, six NADH and two FADH two: two
plus eighteen plus four is twenty-four.

95
00:04:27,196 --> 00:04:29,944
Eight plus six plus
twenty-four makes thirty-eight.

96
00:04:30,594 --> 00:04:32,055
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:32,405 --> 00:04:36,047
Many students think every living cell
always makes exactly thirty-eight

98
00:04:36,047 --> 00:04:37,127
ATP per glucose.

99
00:04:37,527 --> 00:04:40,027
But thirty-eight is a theoretical figure.

100
00:04:40,027 --> 00:04:44,434
It holds only if the four assumptions
are true, and in a real cell they

101
00:04:44,434 --> 00:04:45,517
often are not.

102
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:47,307
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:47,307 --> 00:04:50,765
As statement and match questions on
the ATP count, the amphibolic pathway

104
00:04:50,765 --> 00:04:51,665
and RQ values.

105
00:04:52,230 --> 00:04:53,337
Exam level now.

106
00:04:53,337 --> 00:04:55,459
Match each substrate with its RQ.

107
00:04:55,459 --> 00:04:56,613
Pause and solve.

108
00:05:02,013 --> 00:05:03,277
The answer is B.

109
00:05:03,277 --> 00:05:07,832
Carbohydrate one, tripalmitin zero
point seven, protein zero point nine.

110
00:05:08,332 --> 00:05:13,950
Glucose gives six carbon dioxide for
six oxygen, so its RQ is exactly one.

111
00:05:14,450 --> 00:05:18,386
Tripalmitin gives one hundred and two
for one hundred and forty-five, about

112
00:05:18,386 --> 00:05:19,488
zero point seven.

113
00:05:19,488 --> 00:05:21,521
Proteins are about zero point nine.

114
00:05:22,121 --> 00:05:23,661
Here is your recap.

115
00:05:23,661 --> 00:05:25,244
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:28,444 --> 00:05:30,630
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

117
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:31,930
One.

118
00:05:31,416 --> 00:05:33,943
What are the four assumptions
of the balance sheet?

119
00:05:34,843 --> 00:05:35,743
Two.

120
00:05:35,209 --> 00:05:36,932
Why is respiration called amphibolic?

121
00:05:37,832 --> 00:05:38,732
Three.

122
00:05:38,269 --> 00:05:39,608
What is the RQ of a fat?

123
00:05:40,708 --> 00:05:45,191
Next time: why does a root tip grow
like a steady ruler, while a young

124
00:05:45,191 --> 00:05:46,666
embryo keeps doubling?

125
00:05:46,666 --> 00:05:50,474
We find out in plant growth:
phases, rates and conditions.
